{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe downward bias: The model consistently overestimates prices during the initial 10-15 day horizon following a price drop, failing to capture the depth of the crash (e.g., forecasting ~76 when true prices fall to ~67).",
    "Adjust for lag in mean reversion: The model predicts a slow, linear recovery, whereas the true market exhibits a sharp V-shaped rebound starting around day 15-17; forecasts must be adjusted to anticipate a steeper, earlier recovery slope.",
    "Invert sentiment signal during volatility: High positive sentiment scores (1.00) in the history often precede immediate price drops rather than rallies; treat high sentiment as a contrarian short signal in the short term.",
    "Reduce overshoot on recovery: The model's linear projection leads to an underestimation of the peak recovery magnitude (missing the ~80 EUR spike); apply a non-linear upward correction factor for days 18-25.",
    "Account for terminal volatility: The model assumes stability at the end of the 30-day horizon, but true outcomes show renewed volatility and decline; forecasts should include a downward adjustment for the final 5 days."
  ]
}
